※ pl4g-test: compile=error diag=4541

※ Which element of an array is wanted is not a length, a count of seconds or a
※ number of apples, and an index worked out from one of those is nearly always
※ a mistake.  The unit of the question -- which one -- is `¤idx`.
※
※ A literal is whatever it is asked to be and so is never wrong here: `v⟦2⟧`
※ needs nothing written.  Anything else says what it counts, and a number
※ counting something else reaches an index through `⎕drop` and `⎕unit`, or by
※ a `unit ¤FROM → ¤idx` that says a whole kind of number may stand there.

@[startup]
fn main() → u6:
    let v: u8⟦4⟧ = ⟦1u8, 2u8, 3u8, 4u8⟧
    let at: u8 = 2u8
    let e: u8 = v⟦at⟧
    0u6
